      "updated_at": "2026-07-30T09:29:07Z",
      "body": "While working through the `Modules/_ctypes/cfield.c` and `Modules/_io/stringio.c` entries on this list I ran the suppression file against some configurations CI does not currently build, and turned up one more UB site that is not on the list above. Recording it here rather than opening a separate issue, since it is the same class of thing.\n\n**`Python/uniqueid.c:185`, free-threaded builds only.** `_PyObject_MergePerThreadRefcounts()` does `refcnt << _Py_REF_SHARED_SHIFT` where `refcnt` is a per-thread delta and is almost always `-1`. Fix in #154915.\n\nThe reason it is not on the list is structural: the sanitizer matrix in `.github/workflows/build.yml` pairs UBSan only with `free-threading: false`, so `Python/uniqueid.c` is never built under UBSan. It is not an obscure path either — the first line of output from `./python -c pass` on a `--disable-gil --with-undefined-behavior-sanitizer` build is the UBSan report, raised during interpreter startup, and it recurs from `gc_collect_main` on every collection. Over the full suite with all suppressions disabled: 1762 reports, 529 test failures across 61 files, going to zero with the fix.\n\nTwo smaller findings from the same sweep, in case they are useful:\n\n- **`local-bounds`** is not part of the `undefined` group that `--with-undefined-behavior-sanitizer` enables, so it runs nowhere today. I built with it enabled and ran the full suite: **zero findings**, including no false positives from the trailing variable-length array idiom, which was the thing I expected to cause noise. It looks like a free check to add.\n- **`float-divide-by-zero`**, also not in the `undefined` group, produces exactly one hit: `Modules/expat/xmlparse.c:869`, in vendored libexpat, which deliberately relies on IEEE-754 `+inf` and says so in a comment, with an `assert(newTotal > 0)` above guaranteeing it is not `NaN`. Technically UB in C, intentional in practice — so this one probably is not worth enabling without excluding expat.\n\nHappy to open separate issues for either if that is preferred.\n",
